<h2>Finding and Editing Templates</h2>

<p>
    The Templates page shows all your email templates in a list on the left side. To work with a template:
</p>

<ol>
    <li>Click on a template in the list to open it for editing</li>
    <li>Or click "New Template" to create a fresh one</li>
    <li>Edit the subject and body in the center editor</li>
    <li>Use the Variables button to insert placeholders that will be filled automatically</li>
    <li>Save your changes when you're done</li>
</ol>

<p>
    The template editor is a simple text editor where you can type your email content. You can format text, add line breaks, and insert variables where you want dynamic content to appear.
</p>

<!-- IMAGE PLACEHOLDER: Step-by-step screenshots: 1) Template list with one template selected, 2) Template editor showing the subject and body fields, 3) Variables button being clicked, 4) Variables dropdown/picker showing available options. Use numbered annotations. -->

<h2>Understanding Variables</h2>

<p>
    Variables are placeholders in your template that get replaced with actual data when you send the email. They're written in curly braces, like {ClientName} or {PolicyNumber}.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Common variables include:</strong>
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>{ClientName}</strong> – The client's company name</li>
    <li><strong>{PolicyNumber}</strong> – The policy number</li>
    <li><strong>{PolicyType}</strong> – The type of policy (General Liability, Workers' Comp, etc.)</li>
    <li><strong>{ExpirationDate}</strong> – When the policy expires</li>
    <li><strong>{ContactFirstName}</strong> – The contact person's first name</li>
    <li><strong>{ContactEmail}</strong> – The contact's email address</li>
    <li><strong>{PaymentAmount}</strong> – Payment amount (formatted as currency)</li>
    <li><strong>{DownPaymentAmount}</strong> – Down payment amount (formatted as currency)</li>
</ul>

<p>
    When you click the Variables button, you'll see a list of all available variables. Click one to insert it into your template at the cursor position.
</p>

